### Cover both rate-limit statuses without retries (2/3)

**Severity:** `medium`

The existing rate-limit regression exercises only 403, so it does not detect automatic retries after a 429 response. Exercise both status codes through a faulted task and assert one operation call and no delay calls. This protects both the existing 403 behavior and the 429 fail-fast change.

```suggestion
        [DataTestMethod]
        [DataRow(HttpStatusCode.Forbidden)]
        [DataRow(HttpStatusCode.TooManyRequests)]
        public async Task RetryWithBackoffDoesNotRetryRateLimit(HttpStatusCode statusCode)
        {
            int calls = 0;
            int delayCalls = 0;

            await Assert.ThrowsExceptionAsync<HttpRequestException>(() => ScoobeWindow.RetryWithBackoffAsync<string>(
                () =>
                {
                    calls++;
                    return Task.FromException<string>(new HttpRequestException("rate limited", null, statusCode));
                },
                _ =>
                {
                    delayCalls++;
                    return Task.CompletedTask;
                }));

            Assert.AreEqual(1, calls);
            Assert.AreEqual(0, delayCalls);
```

Apply all three related suggestions together as one batch.
